course: dictionary
lesson: 10409
title: One word, many meanings: trifle
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. The words around it, its context, tell you which meaning fits. «Toad hunts out cold chicken, trifle and lobster salad.» Chicken and lobster salad are food, so trifle must be a food here: a cold dessert.
explain: «He is a trifle tired after the long walk.» Trifle here does not mean a dessert. It means a little or slightly, so he is only a bit tired. Test it: swap in a little, and «a little tired» still makes sense.
explain: Sometimes trifle means something small and not important, as in a trifle. Look for clues. If a sentence says «Not to be trifled with», the clue is the warning, so trifle is a verb here and it means to treat something without enough respect. The meaning is that someone or something is serious or dangerous.
explain: When you find a word with more than one meaning, do not stop at the first one you know. Read the whole sentence, ask what makes sense, and try each meaning. A dictionary lists every meaning, so check there if you are still unsure.
fact: Some words have several meanings, and context tells you which one the writer means.
fact: Trifle is a cold dessert with layers of sponge cake, fruit or jelly, custard and cream. It is popular in the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand.
fact: Trifle can also mean something small and unimportant.
fact: «A trifle» means a little or slightly, as in «a trifle tired».
fact: «Not to be trifled with» is a warning that someone or something is serious or dangerous.
exercise: «Toad hunts out cold chicken, trifle and lobster salad.» In this sentence, what is trifle? | A small, unimportant thing | *A cold dessert with sponge, fruit, custard and cream | A warning to be careful | A little bit of something
exercise: «He is a trifle tired after the long walk.» What does a trifle mean here? | A cold dessert made with custard | *A little, slightly | Treated with no respect | Something small and not important
exercise: A sign says «Not to be trifled with». What does it warn you? | That it is free to take | That it is small and unimportant | That it is a sweet dessert | *That it is serious or dangerous
test: «Aunt Pam says a quote over the trifle.» In this sentence, what is trifle? | Treating someone with no respect | *A cold dessert | A small, unimportant thing | A little bit
test: Which phrase below means a little bit tired? | *A trifle tired | Not to be trifled with | Strawberry trifle | Trifled with
exam: Which meaning of trifle fits best in «Do not trifle with the dog, it bites»? | Something small and unimportant | A cold dessert with custard | *To treat something without enough respect or seriousness, so it is risky | A little, slightly
